==============================================================================
[E6] the mechanism theorem (this is the ONE link that breaks)
==============================================================================
For any positive measure nu and any eps>0:
    chi = int s^{-1} dnu >= (2eps)^{-1} nu([0,2eps])   ==>   nu([0,2eps]) <= 2 eps chi.
So H1 (chi <= 1/lam) forces     nu([0,2eps]) <= 2eps/lam   for every eps:  the edge
residue must vanish at rate O(eps).  H1 therefore constrains the RESIDUE DENSITY at the
bottom, never its LOCATION.  Any claim 'H1 => m>0' must convert a residue ceiling into a
position floor; those are different coordinates on the same cone of measures.  [T]
Numerical check (weight in [0,2eps] vs the H1 ceiling 2eps/lam, lam=1):
  E1 thin atom (w=eps/2lam)        chi=    1.0000  nu([0,2eps])= 0.000500  ceiling 2eps/lam= 0.002000  H1=True
  E2 thin atom (w=eps/4lam)        chi=    0.7500  nu([0,2eps])= 0.000250  ceiling 2eps/lam= 0.002000  H1=True
  E3 near-degenerate 1/2,1/2       chi=  750.0000  nu([0,2eps])= 1.000000  ceiling 2eps/lam= 0.002000  H1=False

